From: dev0gig <115336867+dev0gig@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 06:59:05 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] Synchronous mode: input pump instead of waiting for a GUI
 thread
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A human decision in Forge is an InputSyncronizedBase: the game thread
shows it and parks on a CountDownLatch until the GUI thread answers
(selectButtonOk, selectCard, ...) and that answer calls stop(). GraalVM
Web Image has exactly one Java thread, so nobody could ever release the
latch.

With -Dforge.synchronous=true, awaitLatchRelease() now runs an input
pump on the game thread instead: each call obtains one user input
(blocking until one arrives) and delivers it to the controller, until
stop() or the end of the game releases the latch. Inputs that open further
inputs while being handled (targets, costs) nest on the same stack, just
as nested showAndWait() calls do on the game thread today.

The pump is installed by the embedding application
(setSynchronousInputPump). In synchronous mode without a pump the input
fails loudly instead of hanging. Without the property nothing changes.

Written for OpenMana on 2026-09-24 (research: OPENMANA_ENGINE_PLAN.md
§3.3, "Patch 4").
